<stack-static>
    <statement><p><p><m>A</m> is <m>({-6}, {-5})</m> and <m>B</m> is <m>({0}, {-3})</m>. The locus of points equidistant from <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> is drawn. For each point, decide where it is.</p><ol><li><div><m>P = ({-7}, {-4})</m> [ on the locus | nearer to A | nearer to B ]  </div></li><li><div><m>P = ({-5}, {2})</m> [ on the locus | nearer to A | nearer to B ]  </div></li><li><div><m>P = ({-3}, {-1})</m> [ on the locus | nearer to A | nearer to B ]  </div></li><li><div><m>P = ({-1}, {-3})</m> [ on the locus | nearer to A | nearer to B ]  </div></li></ol></p></statement>
    <solution><p><p>A point is on the perpendicular bisector of <m>AB</m> exactly when it is the same distance from <m>A</m> as from <m>B</m>. Compare the squares of the distances, worked out by Pythagoras, so that nothing is rounded. The midpoint of <m>AB</m> is <m>M({-3}, {-4})</m>, but <m>AB</m> is not horizontal or vertical, so the bisector is a sloping line through <m>M</m>.</p><ol><li><p><m>({-7}, {-4})</m>: <m>PA^2 = {2}</m> and <m>PB^2 = {50}</m>, so the point is nearer to <m>A</m>.</p></li><li><p><m>({-5}, {2})</m>: <m>PA^2 = {50}</m> and <m>PB^2 = {50}</m>, so the point is on the locus.</p></li><li><p><m>({-3}, {-1})</m>: <m>PA^2 = {25}</m> and <m>PB^2 = {13}</m>, so the point is nearer to <m>B</m>.</p></li><li><p><m>({-1}, {-3})</m>: <m>PA^2 = {29}</m> and <m>PB^2 = {1}</m>, so the point is nearer to <m>B</m>.</p></li></ol></p></solution>
    <answer><p>nearer to A</p></answer>
    <answer><p>on the locus</p></answer>
    <answer><p>nearer to B</p></answer>
    <answer><p>nearer to B</p></answer>
</stack-static>